  <h2>3 Critical Technical Metrics Manufacturers Hide in Spec Sheets</h2>
  <p><b>1. CRI vs. TLCI (The Skin Tone Accuracy Index):</b> While almost every lighting manufacturer advertises an inflated CRI (Color Rendering Index) exceeding 90, this measurement relies primarily on human ocular perception. The more vital specification for videographers is <b>TLCI (Television Lighting Consistency Index)</b>, which explicitly evaluates how camera sensors interpret spectral light frequency. The Godox SL60IID boasts an exceptional TLCI 97+ certification—ensuring natural, true-to-life skin tones that demand zero magenta or green tint corrections in post-production.<br>
  <b>2. The Bi-Color Power Penalty:</b> Variable bi-color LED fixtures achieve thermal shifting by splitting their diode arrays evenly between dedicated warm (tungsten) and cool (daylight) beads. Consequently, when you set a bi-color fixture strictly to 3200K or 6500K, half of the physical diodes sit totally inactive—meaning an advertised 250W bi-color head effectively delivers only roughly ~130W of luminous output at extreme color spectrums.<br>
  <b>3. Fan Decibel Output vs. Microphone Proximity:</b> High-output COB fixtures operating between 250W and 300W generate significant thermal heat, forcing onboard internal cooling fans to run at high rpm velocities. If you record audio via sensitive directional shotgun or large-diaphragm condenser microphones within tight home studios, ensure your lighting fixture sits at least 5 to 6 feet away from your primary dialogue pickup zone to prevent unwanted background fan hum.</p>

